{"step_index":357,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T14:26:16Z","content":"I have created the KeePass server pool engine and search playbook inside your **[~/.ansible](file:///home/koby/.ansible)** workspace.\n\n---\n\n### What Was Built\n\n#### 1. The Parser Engine: [scripts/build_kdbx_pool.py](file:///home/koby/.ansible/scripts/build_kdbx_pool.py)\nUses the native `/usr/bin/keepassxc-cli` utility to stream and decrypt your `.kdbx` database entirely in memory (no decrypted files touching disk):\n- **Full Hierarchy Extraction**: Traverses all groups and folders recursively.\n- **Deep Reconnaissance**: Scans Titles, URLs, Notes, and custom fields to extract:\n  - Hostnames & Entry Titles\n  - IPv4 addresses (with subnet & netmask sanitization)\n  - Usernames / logins\n  - SSH / RDP ports\n  - Group paths and notes\n- **Dual Outputs**:\n  1. **Ansible Inventory** ([inventory/server_pool.yml](file:///home/koby/.ansible/inventory/server_pool.yml)): A fully functional Ansible inventory mapping `ansible_host`, `ansible_user`, `ansible_port`, and group hierarchies so you can target servers directly in future plays.\n  2. **Fast Search Index** ([inventory/server_pool.json](file:///home/koby/.ansible/inventory/server_pool.json)): Cached metadata for millisecond-fast lookups without needing to enter the KeePass master password on every query.\n- **Security-First**: Does **not** dump plaintext passwords into the inventory file.\n\n#### 2. The Playbook: [playbooks/kdbx-pool.yml](file:///home/koby/.ansible/playbooks/kdbx-pool.yml)\n*(Also symlinked as `playbooks/search-servers.yml`)*\n- Validated with `ansible-lint` (5/5 star production rating).\n- Supports both **syncing** from your KeePass database (prompting masked password securely) and **instant searching** across your server pool.\n\n#### 3. Fast CLI Helper: [search-servers.sh](file:///home/koby/.ansible/search-servers.sh)\nA quick shell wrapper in `~/.ansible` when you want instant shell answers without Ansible startup overhead.\n\n---\n\n### How to Use\n\n#### Step 1: Initial Sync (Build the Server Pool)\n\nRun the playbook to decrypt and parse your `.kdbx` database (by default it looks for `~/Documents/keePassXC/OMT_PFS.SYSTEME.ALL.kdbx` or `~/Downloads/OMT_PFS.SYSTEME.ALL.kdbx`):\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.ansible\nansible-playbook playbooks/kdbx-pool.yml -e \"action=sync\"\n```\n\n> Ansible will prompt:\n> `Enter KeePass Master Password for /home/koby/Documents/keePassXC/OMT_PFS.SYSTEME.ALL.kdbx:`\n> *(Your typing is hidden. The script parses all entries and generates the pool in <span style=\"color:green\">`inventory/server_pool.yml`</span>).*\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Search for Any Server via Ansible\n\nOnce the pool is built, you can query by **IP**, **hostname**, **service name**, **login**, or **group** without entering your KeePass password again:\n\n```bash\n# Search by partial IP or subnet\nansible-playbook playbooks/kdbx-pool.yml -e \"search=172.30.231\"\n\n# Search by service or hostname\nansible-playbook playbooks/kdbx-pool.yml -e \"search=mail\"\n\n# Search by username / login\nansible-playbook playbooks/kdbx-pool.yml -e \"search=superuser\"\n```\n\n**Terminal Output Style**:\n- <span style=\"color:green\">**Green**</span>: Server Title and Protocol badge (`[SSH]`, `[RDP]`, `[HTTPS]`).\n- <span style=\"color:yellow\">**Yellow**</span>: Resolved IP addresses and port.\n- <span style=\"color:blue\">**Blue**</span>: KeePass group hierarchy.\n\n---\n\n#### Optional: Instant CLI Search\n\nIf you want immediate output in your bash prompt:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.ansible\n./search-servers.sh 172.30.39\n./search-servers.sh postfix\n```\n\n*(To re-sync whenever you add new credentials into KeePass, just run `./search-servers.sh --sync` or pass `-e \"action=sync\"` to the playbook).*","thinking":"**Verifying infrastructure readiness**\n\nThe specified configuration files and the server pool build script are confirmed. The script handles data extraction from an encrypted source. It then processes extracted data to construct an inventory file for further use. This process relies on a direct in-memory stream of encrypted data.\n\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\n"}
